Evidence extraction begins by listing overlay networks via `docker network ls --filter driver=overlay`. The attacker’s rogue network (id `a3b4c5d6e7f8`) shows `"Options": {"com.docker.network.driver.overlay.vxlanid_list":"4096"}` in `docker inspect a3b4c5d6e7f8`, revealing non-standard VXLAN ID 4096. `/var/lib/docker/network/files/local-kv/<uuid>` contains JSON keys `a3b4c5d6e7f8.json` with `"IPAM": {"Config":[{"Subnet":"10.0.4.0/24"}]}`—a subnet not declared in the legitimate `docker_gwbridge` (10.0.3.0/24). Timelines from Docker’s embedded BoltDB (`/var/lib/docker/network/files/local-kv/db`) expose insertion timestamps via `ETCD` v3 keys sorted by `mod_revision`. Forensic tools such as `jq '.key |= @base64d | .value |= fromjson' | grep "a3b4c5"` recover creation epoch `1741123456789`. Swarm raft logs (`/var/lib/docker/swarm/raft`) reveal a `Join` message from a rogue manager node (hostname `swarm-worker-42`) with mismatched TLS fingerprint, authenticated via `docker swarm join --token SWMTKN-1-… --listen-addr 192.168.1.42:2377`. Recovery involves:
1) Backing up raft logs to avoid quorum loss;
2) Demoting the rogue manager via `docker node demote swarm-worker-42 --force`;
3) Rebuilding `docker_gwbridge` via `docker network prune -f && docker network create --driver overlay --attachable --subnet 10.0.3.0/24 docker_gwbridge`;
4) Rotating Swarm CA keys via `docker swarm ca --rotate`.
Real-world incidents (DEV-1387 2025) used this technique to pivot into Kubernetes via `docker run --net a3b4c5d6e7f8 --rm alpine` containers mounting host `/etc/kubernetes`.

